x86_64-linux  the case includes <utility>, so clang compiles it against *its own* library, where std::forward and std::move are folded as builtins and emit no function; cxx1 instantiates its own <utility>'s - _ZSt7forwardIR1PE..., _ZSt4moveIR1PE... - a header difference. Every name the program declares agrees (names.sh lists only the std:: ones).
x86_64-windows  the case includes <utility>, so clang compiles it against *its own* library, where std::forward and std::move are folded as builtins and emit no function; cxx1 instantiates its own <utility>'s - ??$forward@AEAUP@@@std@@..., ??$move@AEAUP@@@std@@... - a header difference. Every name the program declares agrees (names.sh lists only the std:: ones).
arm64-darwin  the case includes <utility>, so clang compiles it against *its own* library, where std::forward and std::move are folded as builtins and emit no function; cxx1 instantiates its own <utility>'s - _ZSt7forwardIR1PE..., _ZSt4moveIR1PE... - a header difference. Every name the program declares agrees (names.sh lists only the std:: ones).
